    "body": "Amazon has purchased a vacant multi-story warehouse in Chicago for $195 million, with plans to turn it into the city’s second Amazon same-day delivery center. The facility at 1237 West Division Street in West Town will combine order fulfillment and delivery operations.\nWhat’s Related\nThe company expects to create 200 jobs and begin operations in late 2027 or early 2028. Kyle DeGiulio, Amazon’s senior manager of economic development policy for the Midwest, told the Chicago Sun-Times that positions will range from fulfillment work to management, with an average hourly wage near $24.\nAmazon Flex drivers will handle deliveries using their own vehicles. The model puts inventory and order-packing workers in the same building where drivers pick up packages for delivery to customers.\nThe purchase is complete, but Amazon has not started operating at the property. The new center will join an existing same-day facility in West Humboldt Park that opened in 2023.\nRELATED STORIES: Amazon Adds $1.9 Billion for Delivery Partners and Driver Pay | Amazon Adds Direct Rail Route From Los Angeles to the East Coast\nFulfillment and delivery under one roof\nAmazon calls the planned operation a “sub-same-day fulfillment center,” designed to get orders to customers within hours. The company has said its same-day sites combine fulfillment and delivery station work under one roof. Products can be processed, packaged , and loaded onto delivery vehicles at the same location, reducing the steps between preparing an order and sending it out.\nDeGiulio told the Sun-Times that demand for quick delivery includes groceries and pharmaceuticals .\nThe perfect location\nThe two-story warehouse was completed in 2024 by Chicago-based Logistics Property Co. and has sat vacant since. It has truck docks on both floors.\nDeGiulio pointed to highway access and nearby roads such as Elston Avenue and the Kennedy Expressway as reasons Amazon chose the site, while acknowledging traffic congestion in the area. “It's almost like this building was built for us to use,” he said.\nOther Amazon News\nAmazon Settles D.C. Lawsuit Over Slower Prime Deliveries : Amazon will pay $8.25 million, including refunds to customers, and must disclose future delivery exclusions in the District.\nAmazon Expands Locker Pickup at U.S. Colleges : The expansion gives students more locations to pick up packages at colleges and universities.\nAmazon Delivery Vehicles Got Over 14,000 Citations in NYC : An analysis found thousands of violations, including blocking fire hydrants and speeding in school zones.\nAmazon Plans $100 Million Manufacturing Facility in Indiana : The planned Greenwood facility will produce warehouse robots and create 300 jobs, with operations expected to begin in 2028.",
